// A tail cycle ACROSS the impl/free boundary, at a depth a plain recursive Go
// call CANNOT survive.
//
// This is the shape that was detected and REFUSED by name when the detector's
// node set was fixed: the free half was a graph node and the impl half was not,
// so the edge existed in neither direction. It now lowers to one shared driver
// whose arms are one free body and one impl body.
//
// The depth is the load-bearing part of the fixture. Direct Go recursion holds
// this shape to 1e6 on 16 MB of stack and 1e7 on 256 MB, so a shallow version
// passes whether or not the cycle is lowered — which is why an impl-function
// tail self-call survived six corpus sweeps reporting `0 RUN-FAIL`. At 1e8 an
// untrampolined lowering dies with `fatal error: stack overflow` and exit 2.
//
// The interpreter runs it in constant stack: `analysis.MarkTailCalls` has an
// `*ast.ImplBlock` arm precisely so that TCO fires inside an impl block.
import std/io

interface Stepper {
    fn step(value: self, n: Int): Int
}

struct Down {}

impl Stepper for Down {
    fn step(value: Down, n: Int): Int {
        if n == 0 { 0 } else { bounce(value, n - 1) }
    }
}

fn bounce(value: Down, n: Int): Int {
    Stepper.step(value, n)
}

fn main() {
    s = Down{}

    io.print("${bounce(s, 100000000)}")
}
